39:Why: on 2026-10-13/14 the owner's own build waited behind two eval builds on the one build VM (88001, `MB_CONCURRENCY=2`, container `--memory 5g`), and the subscription login hit its session limit while agents and evals ran, failing builds with `ai_unavailable` (tracker 103). Everything below is in the build-service image (`scheduler.js`, `capacity.js`) and the portal; defaults are the safe ones and `MB_PRIORITY=off` restores plain FIFO.
41:**Priority classes.** `interactive` (portal user builds; the default for any request that names no class, so older portals keep working), `batch` (eval runs, mining jobs, admin re-runs: explicit `priority: "batch"`, or the fallback by id prefix `ev-`/`eval-`/`eval_`/`mine-`/`batch-` or an `eval:` account; the eval runners send `priority: "batch"` themselves) and `system` (the deploy script's guard smoke). Order: class, then per-account fair share (the account with fewer builds running first, ties to the account served least recently = round robin), then FIFO. The portal sends an opaque account key (`u:` + 16 hex of a salted sha256 of the user id), never the id.
43:**Reservation.** Batch may run on at most `MB_CONCURRENCY - MB_RESERVE_INTERACTIVE` slots (default 2 - 1 = 1), so a user build always has a free slot within one build's time. The price at concurrency 2: an eval runs one build at a time (`queue-sim` shows the eval batch finishing later than with FIFO). The reserve is clamped to concurrency - 1; at `MB_CONCURRENCY=1` nothing can be reserved and `/health capacity.warnings` says so. **Starvation guard:** a batch build waiting `MB_BATCH_STARVE_MIN` (30) ranks ahead of new interactive work, on non-reserved slots only.
